// PAIR 4 of 6, CASUALTY GUARD — `(ref a, b)` under a deref scrutinee is LEGAL,
// and the first correct spelling of arm 4 refused it (MEASURED rc=1). Two
// separate reasons, both measured with the walk instrumented rather than read:
//  · build_pattern's PAT_WILD tuple-element arm rebuilt `ref a` as a bare named
//    Wild, so the keyword was GONE by the time the walk saw it — `(ref a, b)`
//    and `(a, b)` were the same nodes. The Wild claim is therefore trusted only
//    at the arm's ROOT, never under a Tuple.
//    ⚠ CORRECTED 2026-09-06h: that first bullet is no longer true of the tree.
//    The tuple door now mints a PatRefBind sub for a written `ref`/`ref mut`,
//    so the keyword DOES survive and the walk reads its mode off that node.
//    This file stayed GREEN across that landing, which is the guard doing its
//    job in the other direction: what it forbade was a HALF-carried fact, and
//    the fact is now whole. The Wild-claim rule above is unchanged and is still
//    what keeps a by-VALUE tuple element from being read as a borrow.
//  · `bind_ref_modes()` is minted ONLY where a mode is spelled: `E::A(d)` walks
//    with modes=0 and `E::A(ref d)` with modes=1. An EMPTY vector means "all by
//    value", not "unknown" — reading it as unknown silenced all three rows.
// If this file ever reddens, the walk has started claiming a fact the tree does
// not carry.
package bc_patmovebind_tuple_ref_element_twin;
struct P { n: i64 }
impl Drop for P { fn drop(self: &mut P) {} }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let t: (P, i64) = (P { n: 1i64 }, 2i64);
    let r: &(P, i64) = &t;
    match *r {
        (ref a, b) => {
            let v: i64 = a.n + b;
            return (v - 3i64) as i32;
        }
    }
}
